{"id": "M7-c056", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is the dual function g(λ, ν)?", "back": "g(λ, ν) = inf over x in R^n of L(x, λ, ν), a number or −∞ (Definition 1.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c057", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Is the dual function concave, and does this need convexity of the problem?", "back": "Yes, on the set where it is finite. For a given x the Lagrangian is affine in (λ, ν), and the infimum of a sum is at least the sum of the infima (Proposition 1.2). The proof uses no convexity of f or the g_i.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c058", "lesson": "L04", "front": "State weak duality.", "back": "For every dual feasible (λ, ν), g(λ, ν) ≤ p*. Hence d* ≤ p*. The statement needs no convexity assumption (Proposition 1.3). The proof uses λ_i ≥ 0, g_i(x) ≤ 0 and h_j(x) = 0 for feasible x.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c059", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What bound do finite grids of primal and dual points give on the duality gap?", "back": "For finite non-empty F̃ in F and dual feasible D̃, min over F̃ of f minus max over D̃ of g is at least p* − d*, which is at least 0 (Proposition 1.4). Finiteness makes the minimum and maximum exist.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c060", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Can a dual grid show a positive gap when the true duality gap is zero?", "back": "Yes. Restricting the dual grid of Lab 1 to λ = (0, 0) and ν in steps of 1/10 on [−1, 0] gives a grid gap of 7/300, while the true gap is zero (Problem R3). A grid gap bounds the true gap only from above (Remark 1.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c061", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Hypotheses and conclusion of the KKT zero-gap proposition.", "back": "Assume f and each g_i are convex and differentiable on R^n, and each h_j is affine. If (x̂, (λ*, ν*)) is a KKT point, then g(λ*, ν*) = f(x̂), and p* = d* = f(x̂). The dual optimum is attained at (λ*, ν*) and the primal optimum at x̂ (Proposition 2.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c062", "lesson": "L04", "front": "For the active two-asset instance, what is the closed form of the dual function?", "back": "With r = μ + λ − ν1, g(λ, ν) = −(1/2)r'Σ⁻¹r − ν (Example 2.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c063", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Lab 1, how many dual and primal grid points are used?", "back": "1331 dual points (λ_i in steps of 1/10 on [0, 1], ν in steps of 1/10 on [−1, 0]) and 21 primal points (w = (k/20, 1 − k/20)).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED), exact arithmetic on the listed grids"}
{"id": "M7-c064", "lesson": "L04", "front": "For the active instance, what is g(0, 0)?", "back": "−199/300, about −0.6633. Here r = μ, μ'Σ⁻¹μ = 199/150, and g(0, 0) = −(1/2)(199/150) (Problem M3). It lies 49/300 below p* = −1/2.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c065", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is a Slater point, and what is Slater's condition?", "back": "A Slater point x̄ satisfies g_i(x̄) < 0 for every inequality and h_j(x̄) = 0 for every equality. Slater's condition is the statement that a Slater point exists (Definition 3.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c067", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Does the active two-asset instance satisfy Slater's condition?", "back": "Yes. The point w̄ = (1/2, 1/2) satisfies 1'w̄ = 1 and g_1(w̄) = g_2(w̄) = −1/2 < 0, so it is a Slater point (Remark 3.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c068", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Example 3.4 (minimize x subject to x² ≤ 0), what are the primal and dual values, and is the dual maximum attained?", "back": "p* = d* = 0, so the gap is zero. There is no Slater point, g(0) = −∞, and g(λ) = −1/(4λ) for λ > 0, which tends to 0 but is never attained (Example 3.4).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c069", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Misconception: weak duality shows that the primal and dual optimal values are equal. True or false?", "back": "False. The dual value at λ = 0, ν = 0 is −199/300 (Problem M3), which lies 49/300 below p* = −1/2. A dual value strictly below the primal optimum is permitted by Proposition 1.3, so the bound is not tight there. Equality needs a separate argument, such as Proposition 2.1 (a KKT point with convex data).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c070", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Misconception: the dual problem minimizes the dual function. True or false?", "back": "False. Each dual value is a lower bound on p*, so the best bound is the largest value, and the dual problem maximizes g (Proposition 1.3 and Definition 1.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c071", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Misconception: a grid gap is only a tolerance check, so a zero grid gap says nothing about the true gap. True or false?", "back": "False. The fractions are exact, so the zero grid gap is an exact zero, not a tolerance. The grid gap bounds the true gap from above (Remark 1.5), and weak duality makes the true gap non-negative (Proposition 1.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c073", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is a quadratic program with data Q, c, A, b, G, h?", "back": "min over x in R^n of f(x) = (1/2)x'Qx + c'x subject to Ax = b and Gx ≤ h, with Q real symmetric. Its feasible set is {x : Ax = b, Gx ≤ h} (Definition 1.1). With no inequality rows it is an equality-constrained QP.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c074", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Does a non-symmetric Q lose generality in a quadratic program?", "back": "No. x'Qx = x'((Q + Q')/2)x for every x, so only the symmetric part of Q matters (Remark 1.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c075", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the exact quadratic expansion of f(x) = (1/2)x'Qx + c'x?", "back": "For symmetric Q and all x, d: f(x + d) = f(x) + (Qx + c)'d + (1/2)d'Qd (Lemma 1.3). So ∇f(x) = Qx + c and the Hessian is the constant matrix Q.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c076", "lesson": "L05", "front": "When does the tangent plane of a QP objective lie below it everywhere?", "back": "When Q is symmetric positive semidefinite: f(y) ≥ f(x) + (Qx + c)'(y − x) for all x and y (Lemma 1.4). The proof applies Lemma 1.3 with d = y − x.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c077", "lesson": "L05", "front": "When is the KKT matrix K = [[Q, A'], [A, 0]] nonsingular?", "back": "When Q is symmetric positive definite and A has full row rank (Proposition 2.1). The proof shows that Kv = 0 forces v = 0.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c078", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What happens to K when two rows of A are equal?", "back": "K is singular. For n = 2, Q = I and both rows of A equal (1, 0), rows three and four of K coincide, so det K = 0 (Remark 2.2). The full row rank hypothesis cannot be dropped.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c079", "lesson": "L05", "front": "State the unique-minimizer theorem for an equality-constrained QP.", "back": "Assume Q is symmetric positive definite and A has full row rank. Let (x̂, λ*) solve the KKT system K(x, λ) = (−c, b). Then x̂ is the unique minimizer of f over {x : Ax = b} (Theorem 2.3). The proof uses Lemma 1.3 and the first block row.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c080", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Remark 2.4, why is Q positive definite?", "back": "xᵀQx = (2x1 + (1/2)x2)² + (11/4)(x2 + (4/11)x3)² + (18/11)x3², which is positive unless x = 0 (Remark 2.4).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c081", "lesson": "L05", "front": "State the sufficiency theorem for KKT points of a convex QP.", "back": "Assume Q is symmetric positive semidefinite. Let x̄ be feasible, and suppose there are λ̄ and μ̄ ≥ 0 with Qx̄ + c + A'λ̄ + G'μ̄ = 0 and μ̄_i(Gx̄ − h)_i = 0 for every i. Then x̄ minimizes f over the feasible set (Theorem 3.1). The proof uses Lemma 1.4 and the sign μ̄ ≥ 0.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c082", "lesson": "L05", "front": "For Q = 1, c = −1 and the constraint x ≥ 0 written as G = −1, h = 0, what multiplier does the feasible point x̄ = 0 carry, and how do f(0) and f(1) compare?", "back": "The multiplier is μ̄ = −1, from stationarity x̄ − 1 − μ̄ = 0. Since f(0) = 0 and f(1) = −1/2, the point x̄ = 0 is not optimal (Remark 3.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c083", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the S-candidate for a set S of rows of G?", "back": "The solution x_S, with multipliers, of the equality-constrained QP that keeps Ax = b and G_i x = h_i for i in S and drops the other inequality constraints (Definition 3.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c084", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Does a positive definite Q over a midpoint-convex feasible set give at most one minimizer?", "back": "Yes. If Q is symmetric positive definite and the feasible set F contains the midpoint of any two of its points, then f has at most one minimizer over F (Proposition 3.4). The proof uses f((x + y)/2) = (f(x) + f(y))/2 − (1/8)(x − y)'Q(x − y).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c085", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Why is the feasible set x ≥ 0 of Lab 1, Part B convex?", "back": "Each coordinate of the midpoint of two nonnegative vectors is an average of two nonnegative numbers, so the midpoint is feasible (Remark 3.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c086", "lesson": "L05", "front": "For Q = [[2, 1], [1, 2]], c = (−3, 1) and x ≥ 0, what is the minimizer and its objective?", "back": "x̄ = (3/2, 0), with μ̄ = (0, 5/2) and objective −9/4 (Example 3.6). Stationarity holds with G = −I, and complementary slackness holds because x̄_1 μ̄_1 = 0 and x̄_2 μ̄_2 = 0.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c087", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1, Part A, what is the solution x of the equality QP?", "back": "x = (13/19, 3/19, 3/19), with both residual maxima exactly zero.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED), exact arithmetic"}
{"id": "M7-c088", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1, Part A, what objective value does the equality QP attain?", "back": "551/722, about 0.7631578947. It is (1/2)x'Qx + c'x = 817/722 − 266/722 at x = (13/19, 3/19, 3/19).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED), exact arithmetic"}
{"id": "M7-c089", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1, Part B, which of the four active sets pass both tests?", "back": "Only the set {2}, which fixes the second component at zero, passes both the primal and the dual test, so exactly one candidate passes.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED), enumeration and 33 × 33 grid"}
{"id": "M7-c090", "lesson": "L05", "front": "How many candidate active sets does Lab 1, Part B enumerate for p inequality rows, and how many for p = 2?", "back": "2^p sets, each fixing some components at zero. For p = 2 there are four candidate sets.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c091", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Misconception: a quadratic objective always has a minimum, whatever the signs of its eigenvalues. True or false?", "back": "False. For Q = diag(1, −1) and c = 0, f(0, t) = −t²/2, which tends to minus infinity, so f has no minimizer. Theorem 2.3 needs positive definiteness.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c092", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Misconception: a point that satisfies the stationarity equation is optimal, whatever the multipliers are. True or false?", "back": "False. For a convex QP (Q symmetric positive semidefinite), Theorem 3.1 needs the inequality multipliers to be non-negative (μ̄ ≥ 0) and complementary slackness, μ̄_i(Gx̄ − h)_i = 0 for every i. Stationarity, feasibility, μ̄ ≥ 0 and complementary slackness together certify optimality; stationarity alone does not (Remark 3.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c093", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Misconception: the minimizer of an equality-constrained QP is where the gradient of f is zero. True or false?", "back": "False. With Ax = b, the gradient at the minimizer is −A'λ*, a combination of the constraint rows, which is generally not zero. In Remark 2.4 the gradient at x̂ is −A'λ* with λ* = (−36/19, 11/19) (Lab 1, Part A).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED), exact arithmetic"}
{"id": "M7-c097", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the mean-variance objective f_γ(w) at risk-aversion γ > 0?", "back": "f_γ(w) = (γ/2)w'Σw − μ'w (Definition 1.1), where μ is a given return vector and Σ a given symmetric positive definite matrix, both for one period. The quadratic form w'Σw is the risk term.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c098", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the mean-variance optimization problem, and which quadratic program is it?", "back": "Minimize f_γ(w) over {w : 1'w = 1} (Definition 1.2). It is the quadratic program of L05 with Q = γΣ, c = −μ, A = 1' and b = 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c099", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Does M7 estimate μ and Σ from data?", "back": "No. μ and Σ are given inputs for one period, not estimated from data (Section 1). The Lab 1 inputs are labelled SIMULATED.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c100", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the closed-form minimizer of the budget-constrained mean-variance problem?", "back": "With γ > 0, Σ symmetric positive definite, y = Σ⁻¹μ and z = Σ⁻¹1, the unique minimizer of f_γ under 1ᵀw = 1 is w(γ) = (y − λz)/γ with λ = (1ᵀy − γ)/(1ᵀz) (Theorem 1.3). The proof uses Theorem 2.3 of L05.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c101", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Is the simulated Σ of Lab 1 positive definite, and why?", "back": "Yes. Σ = M'M/16 + I/10 for an integer matrix M gives x'Σx = ||Mx||²/16 + ||x||²/10, which is positive unless x = 0 (Remark 1.4).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c102", "lesson": "L06", "front": "As γ rises, does the quadratic risk term V(γ) = w(γ)'Σw(γ) of the mean-variance solution rise or fall?", "back": "It does not rise. For any set F' of weights, with w(γ) a minimiser of f_γ over F', and 0 < γ2 < γ1: V(γ1) ≤ V(γ2) (Proposition 2.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c103", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Can adding a constraint lower the optimal mean-variance objective?", "back": "No. For each γ > 0, the minimum of f_γ over F+ = {w : 1'w = 1, w ≥ 0} is at least its minimum over F0 = {w : 1'w = 1}, because F+ is contained in F0 (Proposition 2.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c104", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is a linear portfolio constraint?", "back": "An inequality a'w ≤ β or an equality a'w = β, with a and β given (Definition 3.1). The budget 1'w = 1 is an equality.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c105", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the turnover of w relative to a starting portfolio w0, and what is the turnover constraint?", "back": "The turnover is Σ_i |w_i − w0_i|. The turnover constraint is that it is at most a given τ ≥ 0 (Definition 3.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c106", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Is |w_i − w0_i| ≤ s one linear inequality in w alone?", "back": "No. It is a pair of linear inequalities, w_i − w0_i ≤ s and w0_i − w_i ≤ s (Remark 3.3). Proposition 3.4 uses such pairs with auxiliary variables.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c107", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Why is the problem with the turnover constraint still a quadratic program?", "back": "The variables (w, t) enter the constraints of Proposition 3.4 linearly, and the objective depends on w only. The Hessian is positive semidefinite on the w block and zero on the t block (Remark 3.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c108", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, what are the simulated expected returns μ?", "back": "μ = (7/100, 1/20, 3/25, 1/50), which is (0.07, 0.05, 0.12, 0.02) per period (SIMULATED).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c109", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, what are the diagonal entries of the simulated Σ?", "back": "49/40, 27/20, 103/80 and 103/80 (SIMULATED).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c110", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, Part A, what is the return μᵀw at γ = 1 with the budget only?", "back": "0.113798 to six decimal places (SIMULATED).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c111", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, Part A, is the weight on A4 negative at each γ?", "back": "Yes. It is negative at all five values γ = 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, for example −0.524154 at γ = 1, so the budget-only frontier holds a short position in A4.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c112", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, Part B, which asset has weight zero at every γ?", "back": "A4. The zero set is {A4} at each of the five values of γ, so the constraint binds at all five.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c113", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, is the constrained objective at least the budget-only objective at every γ?", "back": "Yes, at each of the five values of γ, as Proposition 2.3 requires.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Parts A and B (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c114", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, Part D, what is the turnover of the γ = 4 constrained solution against the equal-weight portfolio?", "back": "0.5588607333 from the exact fractions, as the lab's ten-place line turnover_exact_at_gamma 4 prints; it rounds to 0.558861 at six places (line turnover_at_gamma 4). Computing from the six-place weights instead gives 0.558860.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part D (SIMULATED), printed lines turnover_at_gamma 4 and turnover_exact_at_gamma 4"}
{"id": "M7-c115", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Misconception: a more risk-averse investor with larger γ earns a higher return. True or false?", "back": "False. The return does not rise as γ rises (Proposition 2.1). In Lab 1, Part A it falls from 0.113798 at γ = 1 to 0.106241 at γ = 16, to six decimal places.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED), with Proposition 2.1"}
{"id": "M7-c116", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Misconception: adding w ≥ 0 changes only the weight of the asset that was short. True or false?", "back": "False. At γ = 1 the constraint changes all four weights, from (0.434783, 0.526836, 0.562535, −0.524154) to (0.388425, 0.203679, 0.407895, 0). The constrained problem is solved as a whole.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c117", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Misconception: the turnover constraint cannot be written in a quadratic program because it contains an absolute value. True or false?", "back": "False. Auxiliary variables t_i give linear inequalities with Σ t_i ≤ τ, and the result is equivalent (Proposition 3.4).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c118", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Key result: the closed form of Theorem 1.3 and the KKT solve agree exactly for the simulated inputs.", "back": "In Lab 1, Part A, the closed form and the KKT solve give identical weights at each γ, and the budget holds exactly at each γ. The identity itself is Theorem 1.3.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c163", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Does Proposition 3.4 need any sign assumption on w, w0 or τ?", "back": "No. With no sign assumption on w, w0 or τ, the turnover Σ_i |w_i − w0_i| is at most τ if and only if some vector t has t_i ≥ w_i − w0_i and t_i ≥ w0_i − w_i for every i and Σ_i t_i ≤ τ (Proposition 3.4).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c164", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Does the monotonicity in γ of Proposition 2.1 depend on the budget-only problem?", "back": "No. The proof uses only that w(γ) minimises f_γ over some set of weights, so it also holds for the problem with w ≥ 0 (Remark 2.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c166", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Remark 2.4, what does the null direction v = (−2, 1, 1) give along the feasible line?", "back": "Av = 0, so the feasible set is the line x̂ + tv, and f(x̂ + tv) = f(x̂) + (1/2)t²vᵀQv along it. The linear term vanishes because Qx̂ + c = −Aᵀλ* and Av = 0 (Remark 2.4).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c167", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Why is 1ᵀz > 0 for z = Σ⁻¹1 in Theorem 1.3?", "back": "From Σz = 1, 1ᵀz = zᵀΣz. The vector z is not zero, because Σz = 1 ≠ 0, so zᵀΣz > 0 by positive definiteness (Theorem 1.3 and Problem P2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c170", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Lab 1, what is the largest dual value on the grid, and how many dual points attain it?", "back": "−1/2, attained at one dual point, λ = (0, 3/10) and ν = −1/2.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED), exact arithmetic on the listed grids"}
{"id": "M7-c171", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1, Part A, what is the determinant of the 5 by 5 KKT matrix?", "back": "19. It is nonzero, so the KKT matrix is nonsingular for this instance (Proposition 2.1).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED), exact arithmetic"}
{"id": "M7-c172", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1, Part B, how many grid points lie below the candidate value?", "back": "None. Of the 1089 points of the 33 × 33 grid on [0, 2]², none lies below −9/4 (Part B).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED), enumeration and 33 × 33 grid"}
{"id": "M7-c174", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, Part B, how many candidate active sets pass at each γ?", "back": "The lab predicts exactly one passing candidate at each of γ = 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 (Part B).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c182", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1, Part A, what are the multipliers λ of the equality QP?", "back": "λ = (−36/19, 11/19).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED), exact arithmetic"}
{"id": "M7-c196", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is d*, the optimal value of the Lagrangian dual problem?", "back": "d* is the optimal value of the Lagrangian dual problem, which maximizes g over dual feasible pairs. The optimal value of (P) is p* (Definition 1.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c197", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is strong duality?", "back": "The zero gap d* = p*: the optimal value of the dual problem equals the optimal value of (P) (Section 3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c198", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the active set of a feasible point x of a QP with inequality rows?", "back": "The set of inequality rows of Gx ≤ h that hold with equality at x (Section 3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c200", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Under which sets of weights does the return R(γ) = μᵀw(γ) not rise as γ rises?", "back": "For any set F' of weight vectors over which each w(γ) minimises f_γ, and for 0 < γ2 < γ1: R(γ1) ≤ R(γ2), where R(γ) = μᵀw(γ) (Proposition 2.1). The proof needs no property of Σ beyond the definition of V.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c204", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Lab 1, how many (dual point, primal point) pairs satisfy weak duality on the grid?", "back": "All 27951 pairs, that is 1331 × 21, satisfy g(λ, ν) ≤ f(w).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, exact arithmetic on the listed grids; the zero grid gap is settled by Proposition 1.4"}
{"id": "M7-c205", "lesson": "L05", "front": "When does Theorem 2.3 identify the S-candidate for a set S of rows of G?", "back": "When Q is symmetric positive definite and the rows of A and the rows G_i for i in S are linearly independent (Definition 3.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c206", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is a box constraint on the weights, and how many inequalities does it give per asset?", "back": "l_i ≤ w_i ≤ u_i. It is two linear inequalities for each asset (Definition 3.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c207", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is a sector constraint?", "back": "Σ over i in S_k of w_i ≤ c_k. It caps the total weight of the set S_k of assets at c_k (Definition 3.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c208", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the nonnegativity constraint w ≥ 0 as a box constraint?", "back": "The box with lower bound l = 0 and no upper bound (Definition 3.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c209", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, Part A, what is the risk term w'Σw at γ = 1 with the budget only?", "back": "0.123809 to six decimal places (SIMULATED).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c210", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1, Part A, what are the weights at γ = 1 with the budget only?", "back": "(0.434783, 0.526836, 0.562535, −0.524154) (SIMULATED).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M7-c213", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Lab 1, what is the grid duality gap, and what settles the true gap?", "back": "0, in exact arithmetic: the smallest primal objective −1/2 minus the largest dual value −1/2. Proposition 1.4 bounds the true gap from above by the grid gap, and Proposition 1.3 bounds it from below by zero, so the true gap is zero.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, exact arithmetic on the listed grids; the zero grid gap is settled by Proposition 1.4"}
{"id": "M7-c218", "lesson": "L04", "front": "For the active instance, what is g at λ = (0, 3/10) and ν = −1/2, and how does it compare with f(1, 0)?", "back": "g = −1/2, which equals f(1, 0) = −1/2, so the dual value attains the primal optimum (Example 2.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c222", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When is a pair (λ, ν) dual feasible?", "back": "When λ_i ≥ 0 for every i and g(λ, ν) is finite (Definition 1.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M7-c223", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What are the KKT conditions for a QP with inequality rows Gx ≤ h and equality rows Ax = b?", "back": "Stationarity Qx + c + A'λ + G'μ = 0; primal feasibility Ax = b and Gx ≤ h; dual feasibility μ ≥ 0; complementary slackness μ_i(Gx − h)_i = 0 for every i (Section 3).", "grade": "T"}
exit=0
